  "transcript": "# Proposals for backbench debates\n\n## Children's safety on online gaming platforms\n> Mike Reader asked for an early Westminster Hall debate with DCMS to press for stronger protections for children in online games, especially around voice chat and off‑platform contact.\n\nChair: First application: a 90-minute Westminster Hall debate from Mike Reader on children's safety on online gaming platforms. Mike, please introduce it.\nMike Reader: Platforms like Roblox are hugely popular with children. Government research shows 40% of offenders first try to contact children online, often via games.\nMike Reader: Gaming is different from social media: there is voice chat, in-game money, user-created worlds and many ways to move a child into private messages off the platform.\nMike Reader: The Government is introducing protections for major platforms, but I want a debate to press DCMS on the scope of rules, age checks and the off‑platform problem before regulations are finalised.\nWill Stone: Which Department do you want to respond—DCMS or DfE?\nMike Reader: DCMS is the right responder because it's leading on online protections and the forthcoming legislation.\nWill Stone: Any idea when the Government's overall response to the Growing up in the online world consultation will be published?\nMike Reader: There have been platform-specific responses, but no comprehensive timetable. I'd like the debate before Christmas or early in the new year.\nChair: Thank you. Clerks will follow up. We can only allocate a Tuesday morning Westminster Hall slot when the answering Department matches that day's rota.\n\n\n## Ending the unhealthy homes and buildings crisis\n> Jim Shannon seeks a Westminster Hall debate (prefers Thursday) to push for a preventative, cross‑departmental approach to housing that protects health without heavy new regulation.\n\nChair: Next, Jim Shannon wants a Westminster Hall debate on ending the unhealthy homes and buildings crisis. Jim, you have the floor.\nJim Shannon: Our homes and buildings can make people sick. With the Government planning 1.5 million homes, we have a once-in-a-generation chance to make health and wellbeing central to housing, energy and planning decisions.\nJim Shannon: I argue for prevention rather than reacting to poor conditions. This won't need burdensome new rules but better alignment across departments and shared outcomes.\nMary Glindon: Are you asking for a Tuesday or Thursday slot?\nJim Shannon: I'm flexible but prefer Thursday; it suits me better for travel.\nChair: Which Department would you like to respond—MHCLG (housing) or another Department?\nJim Shannon: I thought MHCLG would be appropriate, but I'm guided by the Committee and happy to be flexible.\nChair: A practical point: you have many names of supporters. If they all spoke in a Westminster Hall debate, each would get only about a minute—attendance often falls short of the number who sign an application.\nJim Shannon: I appreciate that—many people offer support in principle but won't all attend. I'll leave the list with the Clerks and understand the reality.\n\n\n## Debate for Islamophobia Awareness Month\n> Nesil Caliskan asked for a Chamber debate in November to mark Islamophobia Awareness Month but was told she needs more Opposition signatories; she accepts Westminster Hall if needed.\n\nChair: Next is Nesil Caliskan, seeking a Chamber debate on Islamophobia Awareness Month. Nesil, explain why this matters now.\nNesil Caliskan: The debate would look at the prevalence and impact of anti‑Muslim hatred, contributions of British Muslims and how to tackle discrimination. November is the natural time because of the awareness month.\nNesil Caliskan: I have cross-party support listed and expect a balanced, well-attended debate, but timing is important to maximise public engagement.\nMartin Vickers: November Chamber slots are limited. Would you accept a Westminster Hall debate instead?\nNesil Caliskan: Westminster Hall would be fine, though I prefer November for visibility. December would still be relevant if November is not possible.\nChair: For a Chamber debate we need a balance of Government and Opposition names. Your list is acceptable for Westminster Hall, but to be considered for the Chamber you should add four or five additional Opposition names quickly.\nNesil Caliskan: The omission of some Opposition names was due to time. I can add them and will contact the Clerks promptly.\n\n\n## Disabled children's social care\n> Samantha Niblett requested a Westminster Hall debate to press for action on disabled children's social care; she asked whether DHSC or DfE should respond and wanted scrutiny of Law Commission recommendations.\n\nChair: Finally, Samantha Niblett wants a Westminster Hall debate on disabled children's social care. Samantha, tell us the problem and what you want from Parliament.\nSamantha Niblett: Families depend on respite, personal care, specialist equipment and adaptations, but support is patchy. Workforce shortages and differing local thresholds mean help can simply not arrive.\nSamantha Niblett: The Law Commission has proposed a fairer legal framework and SEND reform is on the agenda. A Westminster Hall debate would let MPs share experiences and press for a clear timetable for reform.\nJonathan Davies: Your application names DHSC as the answering Department, but many responsibilities sit with the DfE. Which do you definitely want to respond?\nSamantha Niblett: It's a tough call. This is about social care for disabled children, so either DHSC or DfE could respond. In practice it's about securing better local services—I'm particularly thinking of a child in my constituency who isn't getting support.\nChair: Understood. The Clerks will be in touch to sort timing and which Department should answer.\nChair: That concludes the public business. We'll retire to consider the applications and the allocation of time; Clerks will contact applicants about dates, required additional names for Chamber debates and departmental answers.\n",
